  "tarball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/oasdiff/oasdiff/tarball/v1.39.0",
  "zipball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/oasdiff/oasdiff/zipball/v1.39.0",
  "body": "## Nullability changes are reported correctly through `oneOf`, `anyOf` and `allOf`\n\nThis release is about whether a property accepts `null`. Changes made through a `oneOf` or `anyOf` are now decided by what the whole schema accepts before and after, `--flatten-allof` keeps `nullable` when it merges an `allOf`, and every nullability change points at the line that caused it.\n\nSome comparisons report differently than in v1.38.0, including new errors where a breaking change was missed. If a result looks wrong, pin v1.38.0 and open an issue.\n\n### CLI changes\n\n**Nullability through `oneOf` and `anyOf`**\n\n* **A `oneOf` or `anyOf` is decided by what it accepts, not by the branch that was edited** (#1420, #1424). A `oneOf` matches only when exactly one branch matches, so it accepts `null` only while exactly one branch does. Adding `null` to a second branch therefore takes `null` away from the property. That edit was reported as `request-property-became-nullable` at info level and is now `request-property-became-not-nullable` at error level, with a comment that explains the `oneOf` rule. The reverse edit, and the response side, are corrected the same way.\n* **`anyOf` no longer reports a change that did not happen** (#1420). An `anyOf` accepts `null` when any branch does, so a branch losing `null` next to another null branch changes nothing. It was reported as `request-property-became-not-nullable` at error level and is now not reported.\n* **Wrapping a schema that has no `type` is reported as the loss of `null`** (#1424). A schema with no `type` accepts every type, `null` included. Wrapping it in `oneOf: [{type: \"null\"}, X]` makes `null` match both branches, so the `oneOf` rejects it. This is now `became-not-nullable`, with a comment saying that giving the branch a `type` fixes it.\n* **`nullable: true` next to a `oneOf` or `anyOf` is read as nullable** (#1424). This is how OpenAPI 3.0 documents declare a nullable composition, and it is read as they mean it, whatever the branches say.\n* **The change is reported on the property, not on a branch** (#1420, #1424). A report that read ``the request property `value/oneOf[subschema #1]` became nullable`` now names `value`, since it is the property that changed.\n\n**`--flatten-allof`**\n\n* **`nullable` survives the merge of an `allOf`** (#1425, fixes #1348, thanks @remcowesterhoud for the report and the two specs that reproduce it). `nullable: true` beside an `allOf` over a `$ref`, the usual OpenAPI 3.0 way to make a reference nullable, was dropped by the merge. With `--flatten-allof`, removing it reported nothing, and it now reports `request-property-became-not-nullable` at error level. `oasdiff flatten` keeps the keyword in its output.\n* **Members that all accept `null` keep the merged schema nullable** (#1425, #1427). When the schema holding the `allOf` has no `type` and every member accepts `null`, the merged schema is nullable. A member with no `type` counts as accepting `null`. A `type` without `nullable` on the holding schema keeps `null` out, whatever the members say.\n\n**Source locations**\n\n* **Every nullability change carries `baseSource` and `revisionSource`** (#1421). Only changes made with the `nullable` keyword had them. A `\"null\"` entry in a type array, a `oneOf` wrapping and a branch change are now located too, at the keyword or branch that changed. Fingerprints are unchanged.\n\n**Misc**\n\n* **`--version` on a `go install` binary reports the release** (#1419). `go install github.com/oasdiff/oasdiff@v1.39.0` produced a binary that reported `oasdiff version main`. It now reports `1.39.0`, and a build from a checkout reports what it was built from. Release binaries, Homebrew and Docker are unchanged.\n\n### Go package changes\n\n* **Breaking: `diff.SchemaAcceptsNull` is removed** (#1423). It was exported only for the checker. The reading of `null` now lives in an internal package shared by `diff`, `checker` and `validate`.\n* **`flatten/allof.Merge` sets `Nullable` by the rules above** (#1425, #1427). Callers that relied on the merged schema being nullable only when the holding schema and every member said `nullable: true` will see `Nullable` set in more cases.\n",
